    "lawCode": "Rpld §29.29 sub 6, amd Ment Hyg L, generally; amd §1, Chap 455 of 2007",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7889\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to changing the\nname of the office of mental retardation and developmental\ndisabilities to the New York state office for people with\ndevelopmental disabilities; to amend chapter 455 of the laws of 2007\nrelating to the use of certain language and terminology when dealing\nwith certain issues, in relation to the language to be used in\nreference to persons with developmental disabilities; and to repeal\nsubdivision 6 of section 29.29 of the mental hygiene law relating\nthereto\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would: (1) amend the Mental Hygiene Law to change the name\nof the Office of Mental Retardation and Developmental Disabilities to\nthe Office for People With Developmental Disabilities (OPWDD); (2)\nmake a technical correction to the Mental Hygiene Law (MHL) § 29.29 by\nrepealing subdivision (6); and (3) amend Chapter 455 of the Laws of\n2007 in relation to the language to be used in reference to people\nwith developmental disabilities.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSections 1 through 30 and 32 through 47 of the bill would amend\nvarious provisions of the MHL to: (1) change the name of the Office of\nMental Retardation and Developmental Disabilities to the Office for\nPeople With Developmental Disabilities (OPWDD); (2) change the name of\nthe Commissioner of the Office of Mental Retardation and Developmental\nDisabilities to the Commissioner of Developmental Disabilities; and\n(3) employ the use of \"person-centered\" language as required by\nChapter 455 of the Laws of 2007.\n\nSection 31 of the bill would make a technical correction to the MHL\n§29.29, repealing subdivision (6), which has no text.\n\nSection 48 of the bill would amend Chapter 455 of the Laws of 2007 to\nclarify the language to be used in reference to people with\ndevelopmental disabilities when new and revised statutes, local laws,\nordinances, charters or regulations are promulgated or published.\n\nSection 49 of the bill would provide that whenever the former Office\nof Mental Retardation and Developmental Disabilities is referred to in\nlaws, contracts and other documents such references will be deemed to\nrefer to the Office for People With Developmental Disabilities or the\nCommissioner thereof.\n\nSection 50 of the bill would provide that no existing right or remedy\nof any character would be lost, impaired or affected by reason of this\nbill.\n\nSection 51 of the bill would provide for a severability clause.\n\nSection 52 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nArticle 13 of the MHL establishes the Office of Mental Retardation and\nDevelopmental Disabilities within the Department of Mental Hygiene.\n\nChapter 455 of the Laws of2007 requires the use of \"person-centered\"\nlanguage, such as \"individuals with developmental disabilities.\"\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe name change provided for by this bill reflects the growing\nconsensus among individuals with developmental disabilities, parents,\nproviders and other stakeholders that the use of \"person-centered\"\nlanguage is important when referring to persons with disabilities,\nincluding persons who have developmental disabilities. Instead of\nreferring to the \"mentally retarded,\" it is more appropriate to refer\nto such individuals as \"people with developmental disabilities\" so\nthat the person is emphasized and not the disability. Moreover, the\nname change provided for by this bill reflects the reality that the\nterm \"mental retardation\" is attached to a significant stigma and has\nnegative connotations. It is appropriate for the agency's name to\nreflect a \"person-centered\" philosophy, to reflect the shift away from\nthe use of the term \"mental retardation,\" and to demonstrate support\nof the respect and dignity of individuals with developmental\ndisabilities.\n\nThe name \"Office for People With Developmental Disabilities\" was\nchosen with input from self-advocates, parents, providers and other\nstakeholders. This name change would clarify that the agency serves\npersons with all types of developmental disabilities and conditions,\nnot just individuals with mental retardation.\n\nAdditionally, the changes to Chapter 455 of the Laws of2007 regarding\nthe appropriate language to be used in reference to persons with\ndevelopmental disabilities is consistent with the overall purpose of\nthis legislation, to avoid the negative overtones associated with\n\"mental retardation\" and to reinforce the use of person-centered\nlanguage whenever possible. As State and local laws, regulations and\npublications are written or revised, use of the term \"mental\nretardation and mentally retarded\" will be removed except for clinical\npurposes as defined in the mental hygiene law.\n\n BUDGETARY IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill would have no impact on state finances, as the agency would\nnot change any stationary, signs or other materials bearing its\ncurrent name until existing stocks are used, and until resources\npresently available permit.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
